      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(durable): add owner-first gateway recovery",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nLong-running and delegated work can outlive the gateway process that observed it. Today, a restart can leave terminal results or overdue work owned by task, flow, subagent, or session stores without a reliable path back to the parent session. The result is an agent that appears silent even though canonical work state still exists.\n\nThis PR adds an owner-first recovery boundary. It projects attention facts from existing canonical owners, persists retryable wake obligations in the durable runtime store introduced by PR2, and only acknowledges a wake after the canonical owner proves delivery or a persistent session handoff accepts it.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nRecovery must not create a second lifecycle authority. Task, managed-flow, subagent, and session stores remain authoritative for their own state. The durable layer stores recovery coordination and delivery evidence only.\n\nThe implementation therefore:\n\n- adds explicit `durable.mode` values: `off`, `observe`, and `authority`;\n- starts recovery only after the gateway has loaded and published its current config snapshot;\n- fails startup before listening when durable authority cannot be established;\n- reconciles source-backed attention facts through canonical owner adapters;\n- fences session handoffs by session generation and keeps queue rows pending until attached-session consumption is acknowledged;\n- reuses existing task, flow, subagent, session, heartbeat, restart-sentinel, and SQLite infrastructure;\n- adds no new database tables in this PR.\n\nThe earlier fork-only PR3 draft was not submitted. During review, generic registry/executor/worker abstractions and inactive runtime configuration were removed. Inactive human-signal/routes code was also removed, and the subagent lifecycle bridge remains deferred to PR5. This PR contains only production-consumed, source-backed owner recovery.\n\n## User Impact\n\nWith durable authority enabled, gateway restart recovery can rediscover overdue or terminal work and reliably hand attention back to the owning parent session. Durable session queue entries survive restart and are not acknowledged merely because they were reloaded into memory.\n\nDefault behavior is unchanged because `durable.mode` defaults to `off`. This PR does not claim end-to-end user delivery after every external transport failure; its proof boundary is canonical owner delivery or persistent session-queue acceptance. It also does not add a generic workflow engine or replace canonical OpenClaw owners.\n\n## Stack Context\n\n- PR1 RFC: #107375\n- PR2 storage and invariants: #111121\n- PR3: owner-first gateway recovery (this PR)\n- Submission gate: latest overall ClawSweeper rating is Gold Shrimp for both PR1 and PR2.\n- Stack parent SHA: `7cda8c7ab829f42ec4a8faf2ed6f62d52596695b`\n- Upstream target: `release/2026.7.1` at `0790d9f593ad30c940ed93b5872a8cf6d6f3cf8c`\n- Exact head: `aa3ada91808b8f376ed03185799fe3ecfb1886dc`\n- Incremental comparison: https://github.com/mjnkao/openclaw/compare/codex/durable-core-pr2-storage-v2...codex/durable-core-pr3-recovery-v5\n\nThe PR is cumulative against the upstream release branch because GitHub cannot use a fork-only branch as an upstream PR base. Reviewers can use the incremental comparison above for the PR3-only delta.\n\n## Evidence\n\nExact-head local validation at `aa3ada91808b8f376ed03185799fe3ecfb1886dc`:\n\n- `pnpm exec vitest run <12 changed and topology test files> --reporter=dot`: 12 files, 201 tests passed.\n- Focused config/task/owner regression lane: 3 files, 125 tests passed.\n- `pnpm exec vitest run test/scripts/plugin-sdk-surface-report.test.ts test/package-scripts.test.ts --reporter=dot`: 2 files, 21 tests passed.\n- `pnpm tsgo:all`: passed.\n- Type-aware Oxlint on all files changed after review: passed.\n- Oxfmt check on all files changed after review: passed.\n- `pnpm check:durable-canonical-naming`: passed (38 files).\n- `pnpm config:schema:check`: passed.\n- `pnpm config:docs:check`: passed.\n- `pnpm check:architecture`: passed, including import-cycle, Madge, deprecated API/JSDoc, Kysely, and database-first guards.\n- `git diff --check`: passed.\n- Exact-head fork CI: https://github.com/mjnkao/openclaw/actions/runs/29677333093 - completed successfully with 103 jobs passed, 8 conditionally skipped, and 0 failures.\n\nThe first CI candidate exposed a direct `durable/startup` import that bypassed the stable gateway lifecycle runtime boundary. The final head routes that lazy import through `lifecycle.runtime.ts`; the previously failing Linux shard and the complete exact-head CI matrix now pass.\n\nThis PR is AI-assisted. I understand the owner-authority, recovery, generation-fencing, and acknowledgement boundaries in the implementation. Local Codex review found actionable startup, acknowledgement, task-handoff, and environment-override issues; all were addressed with regression coverage. A final post-amend Codex rerun was attempted, but the local environment blocked its unsandboxed app-server launch, so the final delta was manually reviewed and validated through the exact-head lanes above.\n",
        "branch": "codex/durable-core-pr3-recovery-v5",
